    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Socrates wrote no texts and is known mainly through his pupils <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, the comic dramatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, whose often contradictory accounts make up what is called the Socratic problem.</li><li>He questioned Athenians who were thought to be experts, showed that their definitions of virtues such as courage and justice contradicted themselves, and said that he was sure only that he did not know.</li><li>In 399 BCE a jury of hundreds of male citizens found him guilty of impiety and of corrupting the youth, and he drank hemlock after refusing his friends' offer to help him escape; scholars still disagree about why he was convicted.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Of all the works written about Socrates' trial, only three survive: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apology-of-socrates">Plato's Apology</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s Apology and Xenophon's Memorabilia.</p></aside>
    </section>

